#{[{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046658} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186436953} :activity/id #uuid "81f168ca-75ef-55be-93ec-76dca916c2c6" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049699 :topic/id #uuid "0c8c634b-da7d-56b9-a349-8bf570a72393" :topic/math-academy-id 849} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186436953 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186401558 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e8434a84-5ef1-505b-a2c7-d42809c26959" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$2\\sqrt{5}$ and $4$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401560} {:answer/id #uuid "386a0708-44f8-5648-9834-907d2d7fef7b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$2\\sqrt{5}$ and $\\sqrt{5}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401561} {:answer/id #uuid "f019f459-9c8d-5bca-ac2e-8bf08a5f03f3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$4$ and $\\sqrt{5}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401562} {:answer/id #uuid "1362416b-6b2e-5b41-8cce-b430bb96c908" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$4\\sqrt{5}$ and $2\\sqrt{5}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401563} {:answer/id #uuid "c489543f-9ba5-563a-8700-e139b5d4d02f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$4\\sqrt{5}$ and $\\sqrt{5}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401564}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186401563} :answer-field/id #uuid "a9fd9c31-b922-5bc4-b3d4-983574117b6f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401559}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "8cb21ae0-f2f0-5250-97c3-5094275c61e1" :question/math-academy-id "q-16465" :question/problem "For the ellipse \n$\\frac{(x+1)^{2}}{5}+\\frac{(y+3)^{2}}{20}=1$, find the lengths of its major and minor axes." :question/worked-solution "Remember that the general equation of an ellipse centered at $(h,k)$ with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{(x-h)^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{(y-k)^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn the given equation, we have $h=-1$ and $k=-3,$ so the center is located at $(-1,-3).$\n\nIn the given equation, we also have ${a}^{2}=5$ and ${b}^{2}=20.$\n\n- Since ${a}^{2}=5,$ the length of the horizontal radius must be $a=\\sqrt{5}.$\n\n- Since ${b}^{2}=20,$ the length of the vertical radius must be $b=\\sqrt{20}=2\\sqrt{5}.$\n\nSo the semi-major axis has length $b=2\\sqrt{5},$ and the semi-minor axis has length $a=\\sqrt{5}.$\n\nTherefore, the major axis has length $2b=2\\cdot 2\\sqrt{5}=4\\sqrt{5},$ and the minor axis has length $2a=2\\cdot \\sqrt{5}=2\\sqrt{5}.$"} :step/id #uuid "c2676447-f702-5622-a1e3-b995f97745d2" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186436955}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186436954 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186294897 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d981ebdf-12c2-56c7-b5f4-eeb735bd889e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293775}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186293775} :answer-field/id #uuid "a6e60596-98ff-5ec0-8a1b-9ea8b40d91ca" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294530} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4109d59a-1fba-553c-aee8-88bf1d81cdb1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293196}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186293196} :answer-field/id #uuid "cc4abb2c-00c8-5fa2-9a85-dde3a949863f" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294654}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "69b56d75-ccf1-51bc-b43e-408c64b94822" :question/math-academy-id "q-277200" :question/problem "For the ellipse $\\frac{(x+4)^{2}}{36}+\\frac{(y+3)^{2}}{12}=1,$ find the lengths of its major and minor axes.\n\n$\\,$ The length of the major axis is \n{{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ The length of the minor axis is \n{{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "Remember that the general equation of an ellipse centered at $(h,k)$ with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{(x-h)^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{(y-k)^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn the given equation, we have $h=-4$ and $k=-3,$ so the center is located at $(-4,-3).$\n\nIn the given equation, we also have ${a}^{2}=36$ and ${b}^{2}=12.$\n\n- Since ${a}^{2}=36,$ the length of the horizontal radius must be $a=\\sqrt{36}=6.$\n\n- Since ${b}^{2}=12,$ the length of the vertical radius must be $b=\\sqrt{12}=2\\sqrt{3}.$\n\nSo the semi-major axis has length $a=6,$ and the semi-minor axis has length $b=2\\sqrt{3}.$\n\nTherefore, the major axis has length $2a=2\\cdot 6=12,$ and the minor axis has length $2b=2\\cdot 2\\sqrt{3}=4\\sqrt{3}.$"} :step/id #uuid "d6715709-7fa0-5c6a-bd95-a26942b6d206"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186436955 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186339078 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0d360012-b71b-5e3d-a5ed-050551fedc08" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{(x+2)^{2}}{6}+\\frac{(y+2)^{2}}{8}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336156} {:answer/id #uuid "363d6738-eccc-55d0-b37d-dc6eeb5492c9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{(x-2)^{2}}{9}+\\frac{(y+2)^{2}}{16}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336536} {:answer/id #uuid "799fad89-4775-5c00-9f34-a99bb77a3ae6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{(x-2)^{2}}{8}+\\frac{(y-2)^{2}}{6}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337125} {:answer/id #uuid "975b13c5-78a1-50ac-8fca-df15265468b8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{(x+2)^{2}}{9}+\\frac{(y+2)^{2}}{16}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337411} {:answer/id #uuid "a0e5af31-ccd8-5042-a136-b3b71e9698e8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{(x-2)^{2}}{9}+\\frac{(y-2)^{2}}{16}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337486}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186337411} :answer-field/id #uuid "e195d8c3-ee86-56e6-9272-e56918978464" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338674}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "82c10773-d500-51bb-9b6a-76b40d3c3844" :question/math-academy-id "q-72252" :question/problem "![](images/28/28079ead5ebb4649900653172d11c25f3ff489c6b34772094a1ba947c18ef3e1.png)\n\nWhat is the equation of the ellipse given above?" :question/worked-solution "Remember that the general equation of an ellipse centered at $(h,k)$ with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{(x-h)^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{(y-k)^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\n \n\nHere, the ellipse is centered at $(-2,-2),$ the horizontal radius has length $a=3,$ and the vertical radius has length $b=4.$ Therefore, the equation of the ellipse is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(x-h)^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{(y-k)^{2}}{{b}^{2}} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(x-(-2))^{2}}{{3}^{2}}+\\frac{(y-(-2))^{2}}{{4}^{2}} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(x+2)^{2}}{9}+\\frac{(y+2)^{2}}{16} & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "f8bb185e-5399-55d1-ad1f-3a1efd9c660a" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186436954}}] :activity/title "Equations of Ellipses Centered at a General Point" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436952}]}
